PLANIFICATION 2 — Byte-code virtual machine + compiler (the strategic answer).

MOTIVATION
The tree-walker pays ~150us/funcall (see [[bocl-interpreter-perf]]) and re-walks
every form on every call; minimal compilation only INFLATES the trees.  A byte-code
VM addresses BOTH axes and — critically — stays fully portable:
- RUN: a compiled function executes a tight bytecode loop instead of re-walking a
  tree; typically 5-30x on real code.
- LOAD: the fasl becomes compact BYTECODE + a constant pool (extends plan-1's
  serializer), so loading is deserialize-and-install, not read-and-walk; form size
  stops mattering.
This is the portable sweet spot: NO native codegen / JIT / LLVM / assembler (those
break "compilable with a standard C17 compiler in POSIX").  The VM is a C switch
dispatch (with an OPTIONAL computed-goto fast path under #ifdef __GNUC__); the
compiler (Lisp -> bytecode) is written in Lisp and self-hosts.

CONSTRAINTS
Strict C17 core (switch dispatch must work with no GCC extensions; computed-goto is
an opt-in acceleration only).  Coexist with the tree-walker: uncompiled code, kernel
built-ins, and bytecode functions must inter-call freely, so nothing regresses and
migration is incremental.

EXPANSION — implementation issues, in order (see vm-*.issue):
  vm-1-isa-and-function-layout.issue  the stack-VM instruction set, the
                                      compiled-function object (code vector +
                                      constant pool + arity/lambda-list + name),
                                      value representation reuse.
  vm-2-dispatch-core.issue            the C VM loop, operand stack, call/return
                                      frames, core opcodes; switch dispatch + opt
                                      computed-goto.
  vm-3-compiler-frontend.issue        MACROEXPAND-ALL + lexical analysis ->
                                      (depth,index) addresses; the compilation
                                      environment.  REUSES plan-3 hot-2.
  vm-4-codegen-core.issue             compile atoms / calls / IF / PROGN / LET /
                                      LET* / SETQ / QUOTE / FUNCTION / THE / LAMBDA.
  vm-5-codegen-control.issue          BLOCK/RETURN-FROM, TAGBODY/GO, CATCH/THROW,
                                      UNWIND-PROTECT, MULTIPLE-VALUE-*, PROGV,
                                      LOAD-TIME-VALUE, EVAL-WHEN, MACROLET/
                                      SYMBOL-MACROLET, FLET/LABELS.
  vm-6-closures-and-interop.issue     closure capture (display / upvalues); mixed
                                      calling between bytecode / built-in /
                                      tree-walked functions (a uniform APPLY path).
  vm-7-clos-inline-cache.issue        generic-function dispatch in bytecode with
                                      inline (monomorphic/polymorphic) caches.
  vm-8-bytecode-fasl.issue            serialize compiled-function objects (bytecode +
                                      constant pool) via plan-1's cfasl; COMPILE /
                                      COMPILE-FILE produce bytecode; LOAD installs it.
  vm-9-debug-bootstrap-validate.issue debugger/backtrace + condition frames for VM
                                      frames; compile the prelude to bytecode
                                      (self-host); benchmark + suite.

CROSS-PLAN
- Depends on plan-1 (cfasl-1..3) for vm-8's fasl.
- Depends on plan-3 hot-1 (profiling, to target opcodes) and hot-2 (lexical
  addressing, reused by vm-3) — do hot-1/hot-2 before vm-3/vm-4.

SUCCESS
Bytecode fasl loads and runs materially faster than the tree-walker on the ASDF
corpus; prelude self-hosts to bytecode; full suite green; still one-command C17
build in POSIX with no extensions required.
